    "body": "# What does this PR do?\n\n`get_dataset_list()` opens `dataset_info.json` with `open(config_path)` and `_read_json_with_fs()` calls `fs.open(path, \"r\")`, both with no encoding, so both use the platform's default text encoding. On Windows that is a legacy code page, not UTF-8. `webui/common.py` and `webui/components/data.py` already open the same `dataset_info.json` with `encoding=\"utf-8\"`, so the CLI training path is the one that differs.\n\nMeasured on Windows 11, Python 3.12, default encoding `cp1254`, by calling the real functions (the same call path `llamafactory-cli train` takes):\n\n```\ndataset_info.json (UTF-8) = {\"my_data\": {\"file_name\": \"数据.json\"}}\n\nbefore: ValueError: Cannot open ...\\dataset_info.json due to 'charmap' codec can't decode\n        byte 0x8d in position 31: character maps to <undefined>.\nafter : dataset_name == \"数据.json\"\n\nread_cloud_json path, a UTF-8 JSON [{\"instruction\": \"你好\"}] read through fsspec:\nbefore: 'ä½\\xa0å¥½'   (no error, the text is silently corrupted)\nafter : '你好'\n```\n\n`cp1252` fails the same way as `cp1254` on these bytes. Under a GBK default (`cp936`) the first case would not raise: decoding the UTF-8 bytes of `数据.json` as `gbk` succeeds and gives `鏁版嵁.json`, a different file name. I measured that at the codec level (`bytes.decode(\"gbk\")`); I could not run a process under `cp936` here.\n\nThe change passes `encoding=\"utf-8\"` at both sites. It only matters when the file contains non-ASCII text: the `data/dataset_info.json` shipped in the repo is pure ASCII, so default setups are unaffected. A user-edited `dataset_info.json` (non-ASCII file names or descriptions) or a non-ASCII cloud dataset on a non-UTF-8 default is what breaks.\n\nI checked the other `open()` calls under `src/` that have no `encoding`: they read binary data (`\"rb\"`, `Image.open`, `av.open`, `safe_open`) or JSON that `json.dumps()` wrote with the default `ensure_ascii=True` (`SWANLAB_CONFIG`, the v1 checkpoint `metadata.json`, the fsdp2 index files), so they do not depend on the locale and I left them alone.\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/data/test_parser.py` runs the real functions in a child interpreter whose default encoding is not UTF-8 (`-X utf8=0`, plus `LC_ALL=C` and `PYTHONCOERCECLOCALE=0` for POSIX), so the tests fail without the change on Windows and on Linux alike, instead of only on a machine that happens to have a legacy locale. Non-ASCII text goes through `\\u` escapes because a non-ASCII command-line argument cannot be decoded reliably under the C locale.\n\n```\nWindows 11, Python 3.12, torch 2.14.1+cpu, pinned deps from pyproject.toml\nwithout the change: 2 failed   (tests/data/test_parser.py)\nwith the change   : 2 passed\ntests/data/test_loader.py tests/data/test_converter.py: 5 passed\nruff 0.15.5 check + format --check on the three files: clean; tests/check_license.py: ok\n```\n```\nLinux (ubuntu-latest runner on my fork, Python 3.12, default locale UTF-8)\nwith the change                                          : 2 passed\nparser.py and data_utils.py reverted to the base 1fded91 : 2 failed (in get_dataset_list and _read_json_with_fs)\n```\nRun: https://github.com/ErenAta16/LlamaFactory/actions/runs/39246924694 (a throwaway workflow on a branch of my fork, not part of this PR).\n\nNot covered: macOS, and a real `cp936` process (see above).\n\n## Before submitting\n\n- [x] Did you read the [contributor guideline](https://github.com/hiyouga/LLaMA-Factory/blob/main/.github/CONTRIBUTING.md)?\n- [x] Did you write any new necessary tests?\n",
